Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and converts a `std::string` to html formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

std::string markdownToHtmlString<const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string htmlHtml = markdown;
    for (char ch : markdown) {
        if (ch == '\xad') {
            htmlHtml += "<br>>";
        } else {
            htmlHtml += ch;
        }
    }
    return htmlHtml;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownInput = "This is a line -\x Here is another line break";
    std::string htmlInput = markdownToHtmlString(markdownInput);
    std::cout << "HTML Text: " << htmlInput << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function filters through each character in the markdown formatted text and converts HTML equivalent of the empty line character (`\xad') into an HTML line break tag (`<br>`). The rest characters are just added to the resulting HTML text without any modifications. The `main()` function demonstrates how to use this function by converting a markdown formatted string "This is a line -\x Here is another line break" into a formatted HTML text.

